  "transcript": [
    {
      "approx_t": 0,
      "text": "If you get work done and it makes you more attractive, but then you go on and you talk about it, will it actually make you less dateable?"
    },
    {
      "approx_t": 7,
      "text": "When people evaluate the face itself, sort of cosmetic work does not seem to hurt and may even slightly improve first impressions."
    },
    {
      "approx_t": 13,
      "text": "Jaeger and colleagues found that women who had minimally invasive treatments were judged somewhat more attractive and more desirable for short term dating, although the effect was relatively small and they didn't extend to long term desirability."
    },
    {
      "approx_t": 26,
      "text": "However, the picture changes once cosmetic work is disclosed."
    },
    {
      "approx_t": 29,
      "text": "Several studies have found that women who seek or undergo cosmetic procedures can be judged less favorably on traits such as trustworthiness, warmth and authenticity."
    },
    {
      "approx_t": 38,
      "text": "The face itself has not changed, only the observer's knowledge of it has."
    },
    {
      "approx_t": 42,
      "text": "This suggests men may be evaluating two things at once: the face, and what cosmetic work is perceived to say about the person itself."
    },
    {
      "approx_t": 49,
      "text": "If the work is subtle and not disclosed, men respond primarily to the visible result, which appears neutral to slightly positive."
    },
    {
      "approx_t": 55,
      "text": "But once it becomes known, some men attach negative social meanings to that partially, which offsets the visual benefit."
    },
    {
      "approx_t": 62.0,
      "text": "So, want us to analyze your face? Click the link in bio at qoves.com."
    }
  ],
